>
  <h2 id="idea">The idea behind it</h2>
  <p>In an interview you get one cold attempt at a problem you may not have seen for weeks. Solving it yesterday with the editorial open in another tab says very little about how that attempt will go. So myleet treats a problem as learned only when you can solve it again, days later, with nothing to lean on.</p>
  <p>Two pieces of bookkeeping follow from that. Each problem sits on a review ladder that brings it back after longer and longer gaps, and each technique (sliding window, grid BFS, top-k with a heap and so on) gets a mastery score built from every attempt you make at its problems. Today, the Plan, the Skills page and the company pages all read from those two records.</p>

  <h2 id="clean">What counts as a clean pass</h2>
  <p>Almost everything on this page depends on one definition. An attempt is a clean pass when all four of these hold at the moment you press Submit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Every test passes.</li>
    <li>You opened no hints and did not look at the reference solution before passing.</li>
    <li>You did not restart the timer.</li>
    <li>Your time was under the problem's target.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Running the tests as often as you like is fine, and so is a failing run halfway through. Only the state at Submit counts. Hints and the solution are always one click away, and opening them is a perfectly good way to learn a problem you are stuck on. The attempt then counts as a lesson, and the problem comes back soon so you can prove it cold.</p>
  <p>Looking at a hint or the solution after you have already passed does not undo the pass. Reading how someone else solved it is often the most useful thing to do next.</p>

  <h2 id="targets">Time targets</h2>
  <p>Each problem has a time target: 15 minutes for easy problems, 25 for medium and 45 for hard, unless the problem sets its own. These match the pace of a real interview round, where you usually get one medium or two easier problems in 45 minutes with time to talk through your approach.</p>
  <p>The timer starts on your first keystroke. You can pause it during practice, and the code hides while it is paused so the pause stays honest. Restarting it is allowed, but a pass after a restart is not clean, because the clock is the part of the interview you cannot restart.</p>

  <h2 id="ladder">The review ladder</h2>
  <p>Every problem you finish sits on a ladder with five rungs: 1, 3, 7, 14 and 30 days. The rung decides how long myleet waits before showing the problem to you again.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The first time you finish a problem, whether you passed or gave up, it lands on the 1-day rung.</li>
    <li>When it comes back and you pass it cleanly on a later day, it climbs one rung, so the next gap is longer.</li>
    <li>A clean pass on the same day as your last finish leaves the rung where it is. Solving a problem twice in one sitting mostly tests short-term memory, so the ladder waits for a new day.</li>
    <li>Any other finish drops it back to 1 day: a failed submit, giving up, a hint or a look at the solution before passing, a timer restart, or going over the target.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The new due date is midnight in your own time zone, that many days after the day you finished. A pass at 11:50 pm and another at 12:10 am count as two different days, because they are.</p>
  <p>Dropping all the way back to 1 day can feel harsh for a small slip, but a problem you needed help with is one you would have struggled with in an interview, and seeing it again tomorrow is the cheapest time to fix that. A clean pass the next day puts it straight back on the climb.</p>

  <h2 id="example">One problem, start to finish</h2>
  <p>Here is how Longest Consecutive Sequence might move through the ladder for one learner.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Monday: they solve it for the first time in 19 minutes against a 25-minute target. First finishes always start at 1 day, so it is due Tuesday.</li>
    <li>Tuesday: they pass cleanly in 12 minutes. It climbs to the 3-day rung and is due Friday.</li>
    <li>Friday: they open a hint, then pass. The hint drops it back to 1 day, due Saturday.</li>
    <li>Saturday: a clean pass in 9 minutes. Back to 3 days, due Tuesday.</li>
    <li>Tuesday: clean again, up to 7 days.</li>
    <li>The next Tuesday: clean, up to 14 days. It now counts as mastered.</li>
    <li>Two weeks later: clean, up to 30 days, where it stays as long as the passes stay clean.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The one hint on Friday cost about four days of progress on the ladder.</p>

  <h2 id="mastered">When a problem counts as mastered</h2>
  <p>A problem counts as mastered once it reaches the 14-day rung. It takes at least two clean, cold redos after the first finish, each on a later day, and in practice usually more. The 30-day rung exists to keep mastered problems from fading.</p>
  <p>The status shown on a problem follows the ladder directly. Problems on the 1-day and 3-day rungs are Learning, the 7-day rung is Reviewing, and the 14-day and 30-day rungs are Mastered. A mastered problem that you then fail drops to the 1-day rung and shows as Learning again.</p>

  <h2 id="missed">Missed reviews and busy weeks</h2>
  <p>A review you miss stays due; nothing extra is added, and the rung does not drop just because time passed. It waits at the top of Today, ordered by how overdue it is, until you get to it. If you then pass it cleanly, it climbs as usual.</p>
  <p>When you come back after a gap, Today will usually be mostly reviews. That is on purpose: they are the problems you are closest to forgetting. The Plan spreads a backlog over your next free windows instead of stacking it all into one day.</p>

  <h2 id="skills">How technique mastery is scored</h2>
  <p>Problems are how you practice, but techniques are what an interview tests. A new problem you have never seen will still be a sliding window or a topological sort, so myleet keeps a mastery score from 0 to 1 for each technique, built from your attempts at all of its problems.</p>
  <p>Each attempt gets a score. A clean pass scores 1. A pass with help scores less: the first hint takes off 0.25 and each extra hint another 0.1, a look at the solution takes off 0.4, and a timer restart takes off 0.1. Going over the time target takes some off as well. A failed attempt still earns a little, up to 0.25, in proportion to how many tests your best run passed, because getting most of the way there is real progress.</p>
  <p>Those scores are averaged with recent work counting the most: an attempt three weeks old counts half as much as one from today, and one six weeks old a quarter as much. Three quarters of the final score comes from that history of attempts, and one quarter from where the technique's problems sit on the review ladder, so a technique full of 14-day and 30-day problems reads as stronger than one where everything is due tomorrow.</p>
  <p>On the skill map the score turns into a level: Shaky below 0.35, Building below 0.65, Solid below 0.85 and Strong above that. A technique you have never attempted is simply New.</p>
  <p>Once a technique has at least four scored attempts, myleet also shows a trend. It compares the score of your more recent half of attempts with the older half; a difference of more than 0.15 either way reads as improving or slipping, and anything smaller reads as flat.</p>

  <h2 id="sure">How sure myleet is</h2>
  <p>A score built from one attempt says very little, and calling a technique a weakness after a single bad evening would be wrong and discouraging. So every score comes with a confidence that grows with the number of recent attempts. It reaches one half at about three attempts, weighted for how recent they are, and keeps rising from there.</p>
  <p>Low-confidence scores are drawn faintly on the skill map. A technique with only one to three attempts in the last six weeks is marked Watching, with whatever pattern myleet has noticed so far, and is never called a weak spot. It becomes a Weak spot only when its score is below 0.7 with at least four attempts in the last six weeks.</p>

  <h2 id="causes">Why you are stuck, and what helps</h2>
  <p>A low score says where you are weak, but the fix depends on why. Once a technique has at least two attempts and a score below 0.7, myleet looks at how those attempts went and names the most likely cause. Each cause comes with a next step that fits it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Can't start. You gave up without running the tests, or no test ever passed. The pattern itself has not clicked yet, so the next step is to re-read the technique's note and then try an easier warm-up in the same technique.</li>
    <li>Wrong approach. Few tests pass, or a pass needed hints or the solution. You can write code for the problem, but you reach for the wrong idea first. The note comes first again, followed by a problem where the pattern shows clearly.</li>
    <li>Edge cases. Your best run passed at least half the tests. The idea is right and the details are not, so the next step is to redo the near miss and look hard at the failing input.</li>
    <li>Too slow. You pass, but over the target. You know the technique; you need it to be automatic, so the next step is a timed rep.</li>
    <li>Forgetting. You missed a problem that you had passed on an earlier day. You knew it once, so a spaced redo brings it back.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The <a href={`${ROUTES.progress}/skills`}>Skills</a> page explains each diagnosis in plain words, with the evidence first, for example "You passed 2 of 6 grid DFS problems. 3 of the 4 misses failed on edge cases." After every attempt, the result sheet shows what that attempt changed in the diagnosis.</p>

  <h2 id="today">How Today picks your problems</h2>
  <p>Today is the short list of what to practice now. It is rebuilt from your history every time you open it, in this order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Reviews that are due, most overdue first. They are never dropped to make room for new work, because a review is the problem you are closest to losing.</li>
    <li>New problems, up to your daily number (three unless you change it in Settings). Techniques with a weak spot come first, then problems you started and did not finish, then new topics in the order the notes teach them. Your target companies move the techniques they lean on up the list.</li>
    <li>Keep-sharp drills on days when nothing is due and there is nothing new to add, so mastered techniques stay warm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Every item says why it is there, for example "weak spot: Graphs, 3 of 5 failed on edge cases", "Google leans on trees" or "overdue 2 days: 1-day review". If a reason ever looks wrong, it usually means a setting is out of date, such as a target company you have stopped preparing for.</p>

  <h2 id="plan">How your plan is built</h2>
  <p>The <a href={ROUTES.plan}>Plan</a> looks further ahead than Today. It walks forward one day at a time from today to your interview date, or four weeks ahead if you have not set one, and places sessions inside the hours you told it you are free, up to your daily time. Without any free hours set, it uses one window a day at the hour you usually practice, or 7 pm until it has learned that.</p>
  <p>To size sessions it uses how long you actually take, by difficulty, once you have five solves; before then it uses the targets. It assumes each planned review will pass, which lets it see when every problem will come back and keep those days from overflowing.</p>
  <p>Each day is filled in this order: due reviews first, then a mock interview if one is scheduled, then a check-in, then new problems up to your daily number, ranked the same way Today ranks them, and finally a targeted drill from your weak spots. Items are packed into sessions of your preferred length with ten-minute breaks between them. In the last two days before an interview the plan stops adding new problems, so those days are for review and rest.</p>
  <p>Mock interviews start once you have tried at least eight problems. They come weekly, every three days during the last two weeks, and once more the day before your interview. Check-ins come at most weekly, once you have a few clean passes.</p>
  <p>The plan is rebuilt from your real history every time, so a missed day moves its work forward into your next free windows. You can drag a session to another window, take a day off, or give a single day a shorter budget, and the rest of the plan adjusts around it. If you subscribe from your calendar app, the same sessions appear there with reminders.</p>

  <h2 id="forecast">The forecast and "behind by N"</h2>
  <p>For each technique your target companies lean on, the plan sets a coverage goal: how many of that technique's problems you should have worked on by the interview. The goal is the number of tracked problems your companies' sets contain for that technique, kept between two and five. With no target companies, every technique's goal is three.</p>
  <p>The forecast counts the problems you have already started plus the ones the plan will add before the date. If that reaches the goal, the technique is on track. If it falls short, it is behind by the number of problems it would miss. "Behind by N" means the same thing everywhere in myleet: how many problems short you would be on the day if you kept to the current plan.</p>
  <p>When something is behind, the plan suggests the smallest change that would close the gap, usually a little more daily time on weekdays. The suggestion is only a suggestion; turning down the budget is always allowed, and the forecast simply shows the honest result.</p>

  <h2 id="exempt">Practice that never moves the ladder</h2>
  <p>Some kinds of practice measure you without changing your review schedule.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The placement warm-up on your first visit only tells Today which topics are strong, which are shaky and where to start. None of its problems go on the ladder.</li>
    <li>Check-ins sample a stretch of modules you have studied and report how they are holding up, again without touching due dates.</li>
    <li>Keep-sharp drills count toward your streak and your stats, and they feed the skill scores, but they never change a problem's rung or due date.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="mock">Mock interviews</h2>
  <p>A mock round is 45 minutes for one to three problems, chosen without telling you the technique, since recognizing the pattern is half of a real interview. Each hint costs three minutes off the round clock. After the round you answer a short self-check (could you explain your approach and its complexity out loud?), and a pass you could not explain, or one that ran past the clock, is recorded as not clean. Mock results then flow into the ladder and the skill scores like any other attempt.</p>

  <h2 id="data">Where your progress lives</h2>
  <p>Your history is a list of events (opened, ran the tests, passed, used a hint and so on) kept in your browser. Everything on this page is computed from that list on your own device. If you sign in, the list also syncs to your account so other devices see the same history, and you can export it or delete it at any time from <a href={ROUTES.settings}>Settings</a>.</p>
</LongForm>
